<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ibaraki Prefecture</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ibaraki Prefecture is a prefecture of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kanto-region">Kanto region</a>, north-east of Tokyo, with its capital at Mito. It was formed in 1871 and enlarged in 1875 from the former Mito domain and other domains of Hitachi and Shimosa provinces after the abolition of the han system. It is known for agriculture, the science city of Tsukuba and the Kairakuen garden in Mito.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ibaraki-prefecture.jpg" alt="Ibaraki Prefecture" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Σ64 · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kairaku-en,_Ibaraki_21.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Ibaraki lies on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>'s eastern Pacific coast and borders Fukushima Prefecture to the north, Tochigi to the north-west, Saitama to the south-west and Chiba to the south. Its northern third is mountainous, with the Tsukuba Mountains at the centre, but most of it is a flat plain with many lakes, part of the Kantō Plain. It has Lake Kasumigaura, the second-largest lake in Japan, and the Tone River, the second longest in the country. In the 17th century the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-shogunate">Tokugawa shogunate</a> diverted the Edo River's source to the Tone to protect Edo from flooding.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Humans are believed to have lived in the area around the time of the ash deposit from the Aira Caldera about 24,000 years ago. During the Asuka period the provinces of Hitachi and Fusa were created, and Fusa was later divided, among others, into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shimosa-province">Shimōsa Province</a>. In the 14th century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kitabatake-chikafusa">Kitabatake Chikafusa</a> made Oda Castle his field headquarters for over a year and wrote the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jinno-shotoki">Jinnō Shōtōki</a> there.</p><p>In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edo-period">Edo period</a> one of the three Tokugawa houses descended from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-ieyasu">Tokugawa Ieyasu</a> settled in the Mito Domain, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hitachi-province">Hitachi Province</a>, and became known as the Mito Tokugawa. In 1657 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-mitsukuni">Tokugawa Mitsukuni</a>, head of the domain, commissioned the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dai-nihonshi">Dai Nihonshi</a>, a history of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>, and the Mito school of learning grew from it. In 1833 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-nariaki">Tokugawa Nariaki</a> began breeding black <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a> at Mito. During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meiji-restoration">Meiji Restoration</a> the old provinces were converted or merged into prefectures: the province became Ibaraki in 1871, and in 1875 the prefecture reached its present size by taking in districts of the former <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shimosa-province">Shimōsa Province</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Mito is the capital and largest city, and other major cities are Tsukuba, Hitachi and Hitachinaka. The prefecture has 2,828,086 people in 2023. Hitachi, Ltd. was founded at Sukegawa, in Hitachi, in 1910, and Tsukuba is a research and academic city with dozens of institutes. Agriculture is important, with melons, pears and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pepper">peppers</a> among its products, and Mito is known for nattō and for Kairakuen, one of the Three Great Gardens of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>, with over 3,000 plum trees. Morihei Ueshiba, founder of Aikido, spent the later part of his life at Iwama, now part of Kasama, where his shrine and dojo remain.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ibaraki Prefecture” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After abolition in 1871 the domain became part of <b>Ibaraki Prefecture</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/mito-domain">Mito Domain</a></li></ul></section>
